{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2619","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2619","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2619\",\"parent_revision\":\"e2148ae1c03fd367547af307962824adbb032378\",\"root_revision\":\"6e9b17036d8cbb5be7263037fcc918cb56e7907f580e68167c20a8d2ff7da88e\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThe grooming classifier assigns each backlog item one of five issue types: defect, recurring-pattern, missing-guardrail, unbounded-design, procedural. This classification influences behavior within the grooming swarm (defect and recurring-pattern trigger root-cause analysis; unbounded-design gets depth=deep on the technical researcher). However no source attests that the issue type changes anything in add-new-feature, implement-feature, or complete-implementation.\\n\\nVerified by G6 gap layer collection (2026-06-08): the type label and any root-cause analysis section land in the groomed item body but no downstream skill reads the classification to alter path length, agent selection, task count, or quality gate depth. The downstream pipeline is identical for all five classifier types.\\n\\nImpact: the classifier's work is visible in the groomed item but unused as a routing signal after grooming. A defect takes the same path as an unbounded-design feature. This prevents type-specific optimizations such as shorter paths for trivial defects (which could use --quick) or more thorough verification for unbounded-design items.\\n\\n## Fact-Check\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:27:35.747688Z</sub>\\n\\n**Claim verified**: The classifier agent (`plugins/development-harness/agents/classifier.md`) writes `Issue Classification` to the groomed backlog item body via `backlog_groom(section='Issue Classification', ...)`. Confirmed by reading lines 108\u2013119 of the classifier agent file.\\n\\n**Claim verified**: `implement-feature/SKILL.md` contains zero references to `issue_classification`, `defect`, `missing-guardrail`, `unbounded-design`, `recurring-pattern`, or `procedural`. Confirmed by exhaustive grep at HEAD 2026-06-09.\\n\\n**Claim verified**: `complete-implementation/SKILL.md` Quality Gate Plan Creation always creates the same 7-task plan structure regardless of item type. Confirmed by reading the `Quality Gate Plan Creation / Step 2: Create QG plan` section (lines 393\u2013414 of the file).\\n\\n**Claim verified**: The grooming swarm (`swarm.md`) does route `depth=overview` for `procedural/missing-guardrail` and `depth=deep` for `unbounded-design` to the technical-researcher \u2014 but only within the grooming phase. No equivalent routing exists in any downstream skill.\\n\\n**Claim verified**: `work-backlog-item` `start.md` pipeline (locate \u2192 validate \u2192 prepare \u2192 plan) does not read `Issue Classification` when composing the feature request for `add-new-feature`. Confirmed by reading `references/workflows/work/start.md` lines 1\u2013100 and `plan.md` (not loaded, but the plan step invokes `dh:add-new-feature` without passing classification).\\n\\nSOURCE: Primary source read 2026-06-09 against repository at HEAD.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **issue type classification from grooming swarm does not route downstream \u2014 implement-feature and complete-implementation treat all types identically** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: G6 task-type applicability layer, workflow-map audit 2026-06-08\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-06-08\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-06-09)\\n\\n### RT-ICA Assessment\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:26:16.689917Z</sub>\\n\\n- Goal: Issue type classification written by the grooming classifier must alter path length, agent selection, task count, or quality gate depth in implement-feature and complete-implementation.\\n- Status: APPROVED-WITH-GAPS\\n- AVAILABLE:\\n  - Classifier agent exists at `plugins/development-harness/agents/classifier.md` and produces a structured `Issue Classification` section with fields `Type`, `Rationale`, `Analysis Method`, and `Scenario Target` written to each groomed backlog item via `backlog_groom`.\\n  - The grooming swarm (`work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/swarm.md`) already routes `depth=deep` to the technical-researcher for `unbounded-design` items and `depth=overview` for `procedural` and `missing-guardrail` items. This routing happens inside the grooming swarm only.\\n  - The `implement-feature` SKILL.md and `complete-implementation` SKILL.md are available as primary sources and both confirm zero references to `issue_classification`, `defect`, `missing-guardrail`, `unbounded-design`, `recurring-pattern`, or `procedural`.\\n  - The `work-backlog-item` pipeline (locate \u2192 validate \u2192 prepare \u2192 plan) passes through groomed item content, so the classification is readable at planning time \u2014 it just is not read.\\n- DERIVABLE:\\n  - Routing behavior appropriate for each type can be derived from the classifier's decision-tree boundaries. `defect` and `procedural` items need shorter paths (fix-focused, no architecture discovery). `unbounded-design` needs deeper verification. `missing-guardrail` needs gate-addition-focused review rather than feature verification. `recurring-pattern` benefits from regression checks in TN verification.\\n- MISSING:\\n  - Exact routing table mapping each classification type to path selection in implement-feature and complete-implementation is not specified anywhere. Requires design framing and human sign-off before implementation.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Artifact Classification\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:26:22.619846Z</sub>\\n\\n- Primary artifact type: Feature (missing-guardrail)\\n- Behavioral boundary: The `implement-feature` dispatch loop and `complete-implementation` quality gate plan \u2014 both must read the backlog item's `Issue Classification` section and alter their behavior based on the type field.\\n- Domain-semantic evaluation target: Acceptance criteria must prove that the correct pipeline variant is selected and executed for each classification type, not merely that the classification field is present in the backlog item body.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:26:26.381530Z</sub>\\n\\n1. Groom a backlog item using `/dh:groom-backlog-item` or `/dh:work-backlog-item groom`. Observe that the `Issue Classification` section is written to the item body with a `Type` field.\\n2. Invoke `/dh:implement-feature` on the resulting plan. Observe the dispatch loop \u2014 it dispatches `dh:task-worker` for all tasks regardless of classification type.\\n3. Invoke `/dh:complete-implementation` on the completed plan. Observe the quality gate plan \u2014 it always creates the identical 7-phase `qg-{slug}` plan (T0\u2013T6) regardless of classification type.\\n4. Repeat steps 2\u20133 with a `defect` item and an `unbounded-design` item. Observe that the pipeline is identical for both.\\n5. Read `plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` and grep for `issue_classification`, `defect`, `missing-guardrail`, `unbounded-design`, `recurring-pattern`, `procedural`. Result: zero matches. Confirm the same in `complete-implementation/SKILL.md`.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Output / Evidence\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:26:30.694453Z</sub>\\n\\n- `Grep` for `issue.classif|issue_classif|issue-classif|defect|missing.guardrail|unbounded.design|recurring.pattern` in `plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` \u2192 zero matches.\\n- Same grep in `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md` \u2192 zero matches.\\n- Same grep in `plugins/development-harness/skills/add-new-feature/` \u2192 zero matches.\\n- `Grep` in `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/swarm.md` for these terms \u2192 matches ONLY in grooming-phase context (depth parameter for technical-researcher, classifier decision tree), never in planning or implementation routing context.\\n- `complete-implementation/SKILL.md` Quality Gate Plan Creation section always creates the same 7-task plan (`T0: Multi-Perspective Review`, `T1: Code Review`, `T2: Feature Verification`, `T3: Integration Check`, `T4: Documentation Drift Audit`, `T5: Documentation Update`, `T6: Context Refinement`) \u2014 no branching on classification.\\n- `implement-feature/SKILL.md` dispatch loop references only `autonomy_mode` for gate selection \u2014 classification type is not a dispatch parameter.\\n- Verified 2026-06-09 against source at HEAD.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:26:34.348284Z</sub>\\n\\n5/10 \u2014 The classification machinery exists and is producing correct output but the signal is discarded after grooming. This is a missing connection between two working systems, not a broken system. Impact is efficiency loss (a trivial defect follows the same pipeline as a complex design) and missed opportunity for type-specific optimizations. P2 is appropriate \u2014 real cost exists but no current workflow is broken.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:26:37.984688Z</sub>\\n\\n- Blocks: No workflow is blocked. A `defect` item travels a fully redundant feature-discovery + multi-perspective-review path when a targeted 3-phase fix-cycle path would suffice.\\n- Bottleneck: `unbounded-design` items get no additional verification depth in `complete-implementation` even though they carry the highest risk of under-specified acceptance criteria. `procedural` items incur T0 Multi-Perspective Review overhead that adds no value for a naming change or format fix.\\n- Missed savings: `defect` and `procedural` items could use the proportional quality gate path or a shorter SAM plan. `unbounded-design` items could get a deeper TN verification check. Neither optimization fires.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:27:24.718905Z</sub>\\n\\n- `defect` and `procedural` items get a shorter, targeted path (fix-cycle focus, no architecture discovery, reduced quality gate depth) \u2014 less orchestrator overhead for low-complexity items.\\n- `unbounded-design` items get deeper TN verification and potentially additional acceptance-criteria validation steps, reducing the risk of shipping under-specified features.\\n- `missing-guardrail` items can bypass feature-verification phases and focus on gate-addition-specific checks (does the gate now prevent the bad outcome?).\\n- `recurring-pattern` items benefit from regression-awareness in TN verification \u2014 the verification can confirm the pattern does not recur.\\n- Classification-driven routing makes the grooming swarm's work consequential beyond the groomed item \u2014 the classification investment pays off at implementation time.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:27:28.225879Z</sub>\\n\\nWhen an operator invokes `/dh:implement-feature` or `/dh:complete-implementation` on a backlog item that has an `Issue Classification` section:\\n\\n- The skill reads the `Type` field from the `Issue Classification` section.\\n- Based on the type, it selects a corresponding pipeline variant:\\n  - `procedural`: skip T0 Multi-Perspective Review; use proportional quality gates (3-phase max).\\n  - `defect`: skip architecture discovery; use a fix-cycle SAM plan; TN verification checks that the specific defect scenario no longer fails.\\n  - `missing-guardrail`: gate-addition-focused quality gates; feature-verifier checks that the gate now prevents the bad outcome rather than verifying a new feature workflow.\\n  - `recurring-pattern`: same as defect path with additional regression check in TN verification; verifier confirms pattern does not recur.\\n  - `unbounded-design`: full T0\u2013T6 path (current default); optionally deeper TN verification.\\n- When no `Issue Classification` section is present on the backlog item, the pipeline falls back to the current uniform behavior (full T0\u2013T6) to preserve backward compatibility.\\n- The classification routing decision is logged in the orchestrator output so it is visible to the operator.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Desired Structure\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:27:32.035499Z</sub>\\n\\nThe target state has three observable components:\\n\\n1. A routing lookup in `implement-feature` and `complete-implementation` that reads the `Issue Classification` section from the backlog item and maps the `Type` field to a pipeline variant. The lookup must be explicit and auditable \u2014 not inferred heuristically.\\n\\n2. At least two distinct pipeline variants beyond the current uniform default. Minimum viable set: a short path for `procedural` and `defect` items that skips T0 and reduces quality gate phases, and the existing full path for `unbounded-design` and unclassified items.\\n\\n3. Backward compatibility: items with no `Issue Classification` section follow the full path unchanged. No existing behavior regresses.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:28:57.961261Z</sub>\\n\\n1. Given a groomed backlog item with `Issue Classification` type `defect`, invoking `/dh:implement-feature` selects a shorter SAM plan (skips T0 Multi-Perspective Review at minimum) and the orchestrator output states the selected variant name. The full T0\u2013T6 plan is NOT created for `defect` items.\\n2. Given a groomed backlog item with `Issue Classification` type `procedural`, invoking `/dh:complete-implementation` creates a proportional quality gate plan (3 tasks or fewer) rather than the 7-phase full plan. The skip decision is logged in the orchestrator output.\\n3. Given a groomed backlog item with `Issue Classification` type `unbounded-design`, the full T0\u2013T6 plan runs unchanged (backward-compatible default). No regression from current behavior.\\n4. Given a groomed backlog item with NO `Issue Classification` section, the full T0\u2013T6 plan runs unchanged. The absence of a classification section does not cause an error or route to a wrong variant.\\n5. Given a groomed backlog item with `Issue Classification` type `missing-guardrail`, the feature-verifier phase in `complete-implementation` is given a prompt that directs verification toward \\\"does the gate now prevent the bad outcome\\\" rather than \\\"does a new feature workflow succeed\\\". The task body in the QG plan reflects the classification-appropriate verification target.\\n6. Given a groomed backlog item with `Issue Classification` type `recurring-pattern`, the TN verification step in `implement-feature` includes a regression check instruction in its delegation prompt. The TN verification agent confirms the recurring pattern scenario no longer reproduces.\\n7. Reading `implement-feature/SKILL.md` after implementation, the routing lookup is an explicit, auditable table or flowchart mapping classification type to pipeline variant \u2014 not a comment describing future intent.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:29:08.772340Z</sub>\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Agent | @dh:classifier |\\n| Skill | /dh:implement-feature |\\n| Skill | /dh:complete-implementation |\\n| Skill | /dh:add-new-feature |\\n| Skill | /dh:groom-backlog-item |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/swarm.md` (depth routing pattern) |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md` (proportional quality gate path \u2014 existing shorter-path pattern) |\\n| Docs | `plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md` (design principles, proportional gate design) |\\n| Backlog | #2619 (this item) |\\n</div>\\n\\n### Files\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:29:05.189417Z</sub>\\n\\n| Path | Role |\\n|------|------|\\n| `plugins/development-harness/agents/classifier.md` | Classifier agent \u2014 produces the `Issue Classification` section |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` | Primary downstream skill \u2014 needs classification routing added |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md` | Primary downstream skill \u2014 needs classification routing added |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/swarm.md` | Grooming swarm \u2014 already routes depth by type; shows the intended pattern |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/analyze.md` | Scope sizing \u2014 shows how classification influences grooming depth |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/groom-backlog-item/references/issue-classification.md` | Issue classification reference \u2014 defines the 5 types and their analysis methods |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/add-new-feature/SKILL.md` | Planning entry point \u2014 may need to pass classification to the SAM plan |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/plan.md` | Work pipeline plan step \u2014 composes the feature request for add-new-feature |\\n</div>\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:29:12.323876Z</sub>\\n\\n- Depends on: Classification must already be written to the backlog item body \u2014 this requires the grooming swarm to run first (already working). No new dependency.\\n- Blocks: Any backlog item that would benefit from a shorter implement-feature path (defect, procedural items) remains on the full path until this item is implemented.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:29:15.912042Z</sub>\\n\\nMedium \u2014 The routing logic requires reading the `Issue Classification` section from the backlog item at implement-feature invocation time, then branching into 2\u20133 distinct SAM plan templates or conditional skip rules in `complete-implementation`. The classification field already exists and is well-defined. The proportional gate pattern already exists as a model. The work involves modifying two primary SKILL.md files (implement-feature and complete-implementation), potentially add-new-feature and the plan.md work step, and writing the routing table. No new agents or MCP tools are required. The validation burden is medium: each classification type needs a representative scenario to verify correct routing.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:29:01.572346Z</sub>\\n\\nThe development harness's stated design principle (from `plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md`) is that \\\"the harness owns the process\\\" and that \\\"every stage produces an MCP-registered artifact\\\" as a stateless handoff. The Issue Classification section is an MCP-registered artifact already produced by the grooming stage. Reading it in the planning and execution stages is consistent with the artifact-driven, stateless handoff design \u2014 the executor reads from the artifact, not from implicit state.\\n\\nThe proportional quality gate path already exists in `complete-implementation` for items without a linked plan. Extending the routing to use classification type as a selector is a direct application of the existing proportional gate concept rather than a new mechanism.\\n\\nThe `autonomy_mode` field in SAM plans demonstrates the existing pattern for plan-level variant selection. Classification-based pipeline selection follows the same pattern: a field read at dispatch time determines gate behavior. No new mechanisms are required.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Issue Classification\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:27:39.428091Z</sub>\\n\\n**Type**: missing-guardrail\\n**Rationale**: The system (grooming classifier) behaves as designed \u2014 it produces a correct Issue Classification section for every groomed item. No code is broken. The gap is that the downstream pipeline skills (`implement-feature`, `complete-implementation`) lack the routing check that should read and act on this classification. The guard that is missing is: \\\"read Issue Classification type, select pipeline variant\\\". Without that guard, all types take the identical path.\\n**Analysis Method**: N/A (missing-guardrail requires no root-cause analysis)\\n**Scenario Target**: Grooming produces an Issue Classification section with `Type: defect` \u2014 but implement-feature dispatches the same T0\u2013T6 quality gate path as for `Type: unbounded-design`. \u2192 After this item is implemented, implement-feature and complete-implementation read the classification and select a type-appropriate pipeline variant.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"ff3aab69d9637fe442b0d01ebe6b24d15c38fad0e339587f9a1278f6d61bd1a2\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPnMkVg\"}"}